\chapter{Mu3AC rewrites and the x86-64 runtime boundary}\label{ch:mu3ac-native}
\section{Optimize facts, not wishes}
Mu3AC uses MuDSL's existing S-expression rules, e-graph matching, bounded
saturation, and minimum-node extraction. Its local optimizer records facts about
\emph{already evaluated} scalar registers. A fact is not permission to evaluate
an expression early, repeat a callback, or erase a possible runtime error.

The shared defaults in \api{mull/mu3ac/rewrite/default.t} include:
\begin{rulecode}
(rewrite positive-number (positive (number ?r)) (use ?r))
(rewrite double-negative (negative (negative ?r)) (use ?r))
(rewrite not-nil (not (literal "nil")) (literal "true"))
\end{rulecode}
Here \api{number ?r} describes an existing numeric value. Numeric unary plus
can use that register directly. Double negation can use the original numeric
value, preserving signed zero. Known truth values can simplify negation.
The original value-producing operations and source ticks remain in place.

\begin{MuCaution}[Floating-point identities are not integer algebra]
The defaults do not rewrite \api{x + 0}, \api{x * 0}, or reassociate arithmetic.
Besides dynamic type checks and effects, binary64 rounding, signed zero, and
nonfinite-result errors constrain valid transformations.
\end{MuCaution}

Facts are discarded at control-flow boundaries and conservatively invalidated
when a register is reassigned. Each candidate has bounded saturation: defaults
are eight iterations, 512 nodes, and 1024 matches. \api{Rewriter.changes}
reports replaced instructions. \api{loadRules} transactionally replaces a parsed
rule set, bounded to one MiB. Supported replacement forms are a register use or
a nil/Boolean literal. Custom rules are trusted extensions; accepting their
syntax is not a proof of equivalence.

The C++ and D defaults are reproducible snapshots:
\begin{code}
python3 mudsl/mull/mu3ac/rewrite/embed.py
python3 mudsl/mull/mu3ac/rewrite/embed.py --check
\end{code}
The corresponding CTest checks drift before a rule change can silently split
the two implementations.

\section{A deliberately simple native emitter}
The first target is Linux x86-64, ELF, System V AMD64. The emitter writes real
function bodies, branches, scope transitions, calls, and returns in GNU assembler
syntax. It does not embed the entry source for later \api{Runtime.run}.
Virtual handle registers occupy stack slots; bounded scratch storage holds
call arguments. Dynamic operations call helpers implemented with the chosen
host's existing \api{Value} representation.

This is a correctness-oriented baseline, not a claim that native execution is
always faster than tree evaluation. Stack-slot allocation and helper calls
leave clear opportunities for later register allocation, scalar specialization,
and measured optimization. Such work must preserve the same checks and effect
order before performance claims are meaningful.

The emitter maintains call-site stack alignment and the callee-saved frame
pointer. CFI descriptions allow C++ or D errors to unwind through generated
frames. Length-bearing byte views preserve embedded NULs in strings and record
keys. A non-executable-stack note avoids requesting executable stack memory.
Register ranges, branch labels, function references, and parameter/consumer
balance are checked before assembly is accepted for output.

\section{The private runtime ABI}
Objects export \api{mu3ac_entry} and a versioned \api{mu3ac_config}. The current
native configuration occupies 136 bytes and includes source identity, limits,
package paths/preloads, foreign bindings, rule-directory information, and flags.
The private \api{mu3ac_*} helper ABI is implemented independently in
\api{Runtime.cpp} and \api{Runtime.d}; matching \api{Main.cpp}/\api{Main.d}
files provide process startup. The CLI links the selected runtime archive.

A generated object is an entry module, not a general multi-module or portable
bytecode format. Do not mix private native ABI versions. A native executable
also still depends on its platform libraries and any discovered package files,
custom MuLLIER rule directory, or shared foreign library that it uses.

\section{Crossing into packages and callbacks}
Imported source packages currently use the existing host interpreter. Their
returned records and closures remain ordinary MuLL values, so compiled entry
functions can call package closures and native callbacks can call compiled
closures. The core \api{G} services and standard-library loader are unchanged.

Backend accounting hooks share steps, script-call depth, and cumulative lexical
frame budgets across that boundary. The flat native backend does not use the
interpreter's AST evaluation-depth guard; parser/IR bounds, call depth, stack
availability, and native representation limits remain relevant. Optimized and
unoptimized allocations/work counts need not match exactly.

The native context retains value handles and environments until execution ends.
Its additional \api{maxValues} arena defaults to two million entries; a large
loop can hit this limit before the step budget. Native/foreign code is not
bounded in wall-clock time by MuLL's source-step limit. These mechanisms are
resource controls, not a sandbox.

\section{Verification before extending the target}
The \api{mull_mu3ac} unit test checks compile-only behavior, rewrite replacement,
transactional reload, branch/register rejection, and generated unwind/stack
directives. \api{mu3ac_embedded_rules} checks snapshot reproducibility.
The \api{mull_cli} suite compares the existing language fixtures across C++/D,
interpreter/native, and optimized/unoptimized paths. It also exercises real
object/executable generation, process arguments, unusual filenames, budgets,
assignment effect ordering, and third-party C-ABI linking.

When adding an opcode or target, extend both host implementations, the relevant
runtime helpers, and those differential tests together. A successful assembler
invocation proves only that the machine syntax was accepted, not that the
language contract survived lowering.
